WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.360
In the previous video,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.020
we saw that if we have a right angled triangle like this

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.060
with sides a, b and c,

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.540
then the pythagoras' theorem says that

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.520
'c' squared will equal 'a' squared plus 'b' squared.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.120
Let's move on to a new page to understand why.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.140
The process to understand the pythagoras' theorem

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.780
is pretty similar to the one for 'a plus b the whole squared'.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.940
We draw a line of any length and mark a point on it.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.280
This point can be marked anywhere on the line.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.640
We name the parts 'a' and 'b'.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.700
This length will be 'a' plus 'b'.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.520
Next we complete the square with 'a' plus 'b' as its side.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.320
This is a square in which each length is 'a plus b'.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.240
Now we try to make the same division on each segment.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.040
We mark points here, here and here.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.380
The points should be marked such that this length is b, this length is a,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:11.520
this is 'b', this is 'a' and these two lengths are also equal to 'b' and 'a'.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.980
We can see that we have four points, one on each side.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.640
This time, we join the adjacent points.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.460
If you draw this on a piece of paper,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.500
you will notice that the figure formed inside is a square.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.400
Let the length of the sides be 'c' centimeters.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.400
Look at the different parts that are formed.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.960
The outer square is made up of five parts,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.580
This square of side 'c' centimeters in between,

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.580
and four right angled triangles with height 'a' and base 'b'.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.320
Let's zoom out a bit and try to find the area of the outer square.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.960
The area of the square can be calculated in two ways.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.540
The first way is 'side multiplied by side'.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.380
It can be written as 'a plus b' multiplied by 'a plus b',

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.420
which equals 'a plus b the whole squared'.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.760
And the area of the outer square

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.640
also equals the sum of the areas of these five parts.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.940
The area of this square is 'c' squared.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.560
Observe that each of these triangles will have the same area

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.380
as their height and base is the same.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:32.120
Area of each triangle is 'half ab'. To include the area of four triangles,

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.420
we just multiply this with four.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.860
Their total area would be 4 multiplied by 'half ab'.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.120
'2 times 2 is 4' and we get the area of the outer square

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.460
as 'c squared plus 2ab'.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.340
Both these are the area of the outer square.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.120
Equating these two, we can write 'c squared plus 2ab'

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.460
equals 'a plus b the whole squared'.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.220
And using the identity for 'a plus b the whole squared'

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.220
we write this as 'c squared plus 2ab'

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.320
equals 'a squared plus 2ab plus b squared'.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.180
And cancelling out 2ab from both the sides,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.260
we get 'c' squared equal to 'a squared plus b squared'.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.580
This is the pythagoras' theorem.

